sequenceDiagram
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, certificate signing is the one step-to-Gateway call. docs/reference/routes.md#change-an-explicit-domain.
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Node work of the site child is one Ansible phase (see route:create).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: route:update
  %% kind: route.update
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as Route Nodes<br/>workload · Router
  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS
  CLI->>GW: route:update ROUTE<br/>at least one option
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit route:update ROUTE --domain=DOMAIN`
  %%> - syntax: `<route> [--domain=DOMAIN] [--publication=private|public] [--web-root=PATH] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Route and at least one change; `--web-root` is separate from the others
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, lock, record<br/>replacement pending
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the Route's Node, or to any member Node for a Cluster-scoped Route
  %%> - the Route is an app Route (`route.kind_unsupported`); a generated Route cannot change its domain (`route.domain_immutable`); a Route with a web root keeps its domain (`route.web_root_domain_immutable`)
  %%> - the new domain is free and not reserved (`route.domain_conflict`), and no other domain change runs (`route.domain_change_conflict`)
  %%> - public publication needs production targets (`route.reconciliation_required`)
  %%> - no other operation holds the projection lock; Orbit waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `app-dev.projection_busy`
  %%> Result:
  %%> - a domain change records a replacement Route as `pending`; the current Route stays authoritative until the cutover
  alt Node work needed
    Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state
    %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first
    %%> Not held:
    %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read
    %%> Limit:
    %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run
    %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled
    %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes
    %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call
    %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-route-update
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the task ID, at once
    %%> Next:
    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
    opt --domain
      SW->>N: Make a CSR on the Node<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node
      SW->>GW: Sign the CSR<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign
      %%> Why a call to the Gateway:
      %%> - certificate signing is the one allowed step-to-Gateway call; the Orbit CA stays in the Gateway
      GW-->>SW: Signed certificate<br/>Orbit CA key stays in Gateway
    end
    SW->>N: Install cert, publish site<br/>child route-create-site · Ansible
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - publishes the replacement site beside the old one; Caddy reloads after validation
    %%> - for `--web-root`, prepares the workload for the new root (Caddy access, PHP pool) and publishes with it
    opt --domain
      opt Laravel target
        SW->>N: Write the new APP_URL<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/line
      end
    end
    SW->>N: Probe the site over TLS<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · SNI domain
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the probe verifies the hostname and the Orbit CA; an application HTTP error does not fail it, and a failed probe stops the cutover
    opt --publication, or a domain change on a public Route
      SW->>N: Open or close the edge<br/>Ingress Caddy · ufw 80/443
    end
    opt --domain outside the TLD
      SW->>V: Move private DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload
      Note over SW: Wait the DNS grace<br/>31 s: TTL 30 s + 1 s
      %%> Why:
      %%> - the old DNS answer has 31 seconds to expire before the old site is withdrawn
    end
    opt a lasting site is replaced
      SW->>N: Withdraw the old site<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent
    end
    alt every step succeeds
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + outputs
      Note over GW: Record result<br/>replacement active
      %%> Result:
      %%> - a domain change makes the replacement Route active and deletes the old one; a publication-only or web-root change keeps the Route ID
    else a step fails
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
      Note over GW: Mark the change failed<br/>rerun resumes
      %%> Result before the cutover:
      %%> - the old Route stays authoritative and the replacement is `failed`; a failed Caddy validation leaves the candidate file and stops the reload
      %%> Result after the cutover:
      %%> - a failure in the DNS grace or the old site's withdrawal leaves the replacement authoritative and the old Route `retiring`; a rerun finishes the withdrawal
      %%> What `route:show` shows:
      %%> - for a domain change, both Routes with `replacement_step`, `failed_step` and `error_code`; otherwise the one Route with `failed_step` and `error_code`
      %%> On the Nodes:
      %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - run the same request again to resume; a different domain or publication request changes nothing
    end
    loop every 2 s until terminal
      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
      GW-->>CLI: status + result
    end
  else nothing to do on a Node
    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>nothing to change
    %%> When:
    %%> - the domain, publication or web root already has the requested value, or the Route is not yet published, so the replacement is created at once
    %%> Skipped:
    %%> - an identical request is reported as `Skipped — nothing to change (route.update_unchanged)`; no run starts
  end
